INT. MUNICIPAL PIGEON CENSUS OFFICE - SUBBASEMENT - DAY

A windowless room under City Hall, lit by one fluorescent tube that has been dying since the last administration. Clipboards hang from nails like pelts. On every wall, laminated maps of the city are stuck with coloured pins, each pin a flock nobody has visited in years.

GWENDOLYN PRATT, fifties, Regional Avian Statistics Director, stands on a wobbling chair in a cardigan the colour of weak tea. She holds a single grey feather aloft like a verdict.

Around the long table, the team has gone still.

TOBIAS NKEMELU, thirties, the only person in the office with a tan, lowers his sandwich. ANJALI VERMA, twenties, sharp-eyed, clicks her pen once, twice. OSKAR LINDQVIST, sixties, a man built entirely of corduroy, does not look up from his crossword. MARISOL DUARTE, forties, the department's tired heart, sits with her coat still on.

GWENDOLYN
(to everyone)
Colleagues. The Mayor's office wants the Q3 count by four o'clock. We have counted, by my reckoning, eleven pigeons.

TOBIAS
Eleven in total? In the city?

GWENDOLYN
Eleven that are confirmed to be pigeons and not, as Oskar's report suggested, "a mood."

OSKAR
(not looking up)
Seven letters. "Regret, in a hat."

ANJALI
(to Gwendolyn)
Ma'am, I've been telling you since March. The spreadsheet's counting the same bird. Reginald. He's got a limp.

GWENDOLYN
Reginald is a limp. Reginald is not a flock.

Gwendolyn steps down from the chair. It rocks, then forgives her. She smooths the cardigan and, for the first time, looks tired.

GWENDOLYN (CONT'D)
(muttering, to herself)
Thirty years. Thirty years of pins.

An INTERCOM crackles on the wall above the door, a beige box with a tongue of dust.

LEONARD (V.O.)
(over intercom, bright)
Hello, subbasement! Leonard from Facilities. Quick heads up, the four o'clock deadline has been moved to "whenever." The Mayor has discovered squirrels.

The room exhales as one body. Tobias resumes his sandwich. Anjali caps her pen.

MARISOL
(to Leonard's box)
Thank you, Leonard.

LEONARD (V.O.)
(over intercom)
Also, someone's been leaving feathers in the lift. Just flagging.

Gwendolyn looks at the feather in her hand. She walks to the nearest map and, with great ceremony, pins it to the middle of the city.

GWENDOLYN
(quietly, to Marisol)
Put that down as twelve.

MARISOL
It's a feather.

GWENDOLYN
It's a pigeon in spirit.

Oskar fills in a word. He nods, satisfied, at no one at all.

OSKAR
(to himself)
Hat.
